{"causes": ["The Berlin Crisis of 1961 was a direct consequence of the post-World War II division of Europe, with the Soviet Union seeking to consolidate its control over Eastern Europe and the Western powers determined to maintain the status quo.", "The crisis was also fueled by the failed Soviet attempt to overthrow the government of West Berlin in the 1950s, which had left the Soviet Union seething with resentment and seeking revenge.", "Furthermore, the crisis was exacerbated by the presence of over a million Soviet troops in East Germany, which made the Soviet Union a major military power in Eastern Europe."], "effects": ["The Berlin Crisis of 1961 led directly to the construction of the Berlin Wall, a physical barrier that effectively cut off East and West Berlin and stranded thousands of refugees.", "The crisis also marked a new era of East-West tensions, as the Soviet Union and the Western powers engaged in a series of intense and often perilous negotiations over the future of Europe and the world order.", "In the end, the crisis would set in motion a chain of events that would ultimately lead to the reunification of Germany and the collapse of the Soviet Empire."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "cold-war-of-1945-1991", "sourceName": "Cold War", "verb": "FRAMES", "targetSlug": "berlin-crisis-of-1961", "targetName": "Berlin Crisis of 1961", "context": "The Berlin Crisis of 1961 was a pivotal moment in the global struggle between the United States and the Soviet Union during the Cold War."}, {"sourceSlug": "soviet-union", "sourceName": "Soviet Union", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "berlin-crisis-of-1961", "targetName": "Berlin Crisis of 1961", "context": "The Soviet Union's attempt to consolidate its control over Eastern Europe and the Western powers' determination to maintain the status quo in post-war Europe led directly to the Berlin Crisis of 1961."}, {"sourceSlug": "berlin-wall", "sourceName": "Berlin Wall", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "berlin