 would ultimately lead to the reunification of Germany and the collapse of the Soviet Empire.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-19T06:33:11Z", "editorId": "Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct·cloud·GH#27809615080", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "1961 cold war incident in divided berlin Took place in Berlin. Date: 1961-06-04. Ended: 1961-11-09.", "newValue": "The Berlin Crisis of 1961 was a critical Cold War standoff that unfolded in the divided city of Berlin, Germany, from June 4 to November 9, 1961. This event was a pivotal moment in the global struggle between the United States and the Soviet Union, with the crisis emerging as a consequence of long-standing tensions over the post-World War II division of Europe. The crisis reached a boiling point when the Soviet Union, in an attempt to isolate West Berlin, began constructing the Berlin Wall, a physical barrier that effectively cut off the city's East and West sectors, stranding thousands of refugees and sparking widespread outrage across the Western world.

The crisis played out in a series of high-stakes diplomatic maneuvers, with American and Soviet leaders engaging in a tense game of brinksmanship over the fate of West Berlin. The crisis culminated in the Soviet Union's decision to impose a blockade on West Berlin, prompting a massive airlift of supplies by the United States and its Western allies to maintain the city's economic viability. The crisis ultimately ended on November 9, 1961, when Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev ordered the construction of the Berlin Wall to be completed, effectively sealing the city's division and marking a new era of East-West tensions.

